WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.120
When we put together all five of the criteria&nbsp;
in SMART, we end up with compelling and direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.280
objectives. But that’s not the whole story. Now&nbsp;
that you’re equipped with a concrete definition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.520
of SMART, here are a few best practices&nbsp;
to consider when setting your own goals.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:30.400
1. Create goals that reflect your unique needs
SMART goals are only as good as the KPIs you set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.800
Not all business needs are the&nbsp;
same, even within the same industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.880
Choosing KPIs that don’t&nbsp;
reflect your business goals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.120
just because someone else is using them&nbsp;
or a post on the internet tells you to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.960
can send you in the wrong direction. While we do&nbsp;
talk at length about certain metrics to avoid, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:53.280
should always carefully consider what makes the&nbsp;
most sense for your personal or business needs.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.120
2. Use SMART as a form of&nbsp;
communication and transparency&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.040
Like any form of communication, having clear&nbsp;
information helps teams and managers act upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.160
it better. SMART is meant to help provide&nbsp;
a way to communicate exactly where you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.480
and how well you’re doing. When everyone&nbsp;
in the business understands the goals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.520
criteria to reach them, they&nbsp;
can work together to succeed.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.040
3. Carefully plan the best&nbsp;
route, and check in along the way&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.880
It’s a great idea to plan the order of operations&nbsp;
and the order of achievements that will help you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.640
best reach long-term goals. Because SMART&nbsp;
emphasizes specific and bite-sized goals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.280
you’re likely to have a series of goals;&nbsp;
some goals will be in flight simultaneously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.480
and some will be dependant on the success&nbsp;
of other goals. Use historical data to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.200
anticipate issues along the way and plan&nbsp;
for check-in milestones to adjust KPIs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.240
If you have a goal that is time-bound to a&nbsp;
year, you might plan to check in each quarter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.440
Much like using a GPS, check-ins can help&nbsp;
tell you if you’re veering off-track.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.520
4. Remember that SMART isn’t just&nbsp;
for large company or team goals&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.800
SMART is a set of criteria most often&nbsp;
cited as an incredibly useful way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.680
set and track business objectives and metrics,&nbsp;
but they also work well on the personal level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:23.440
Feel empowered to use the SMART criteria for, or&nbsp;
as, an individual or small team. When employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.600
can track how well they’re performing with&nbsp;
great goals and metrics, they can better see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.160
how their performance will directly impact the&nbsp;
organization’s performance on the larger scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:37.680
Or, if you’d like, consider using SMART&nbsp;
outside of a business context entirely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.640
There’s nothing stopping you from applying&nbsp;
this knowledge to a New Year’s Resolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.800
fitness goal, job search, home&nbsp;
improvement project, or anything else.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.360
5. Understand that SMART only works&nbsp;
if the long-term strategy is good

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.920
It is important to also acknowledge&nbsp;
that SMART has two distinct flaws.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.960
First, SMART breaks down a&nbsp;
larger strategy into small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.480
digestible, and achievable parts. If the&nbsp;
overall strategy or vision is rotten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.400
then SMART will only help you achieve&nbsp;
the goals that poor strategy dictates.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.960
Second, SMART is a method of setting goals,&nbsp;
not a way to analyze successes and failures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.480
When setting goals, be sure to build in time&nbsp;
to make your goal setting process SMARTER by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:30.880
including the self-explanatory Evaluate and Review&nbsp;
stages. Always keep an eye on ways to design your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:37.200
next SMART goals better than your last goals. When&nbsp;
you evaluate: think about what pitfalls popped up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.480
about questions you should have asked,&nbsp;
people who should have been involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:46.880
and so on. At the end of the day, SMART is just&nbsp;
a set of criteria and set of considerations;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:58.080
it’s up to us to reflect on our methods&nbsp;
in order to set better and better goals.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.266
In summary, A SMART goal is used to help guide&nbsp;
goal setting.SMART is an acronym that stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.266 --> 00:04:04.659
for Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic,&nbsp;
and Timely. Therefore, a SMART goal incorporates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:08.080
all of these criteria to help focus your efforts&nbsp;
and increase the chances of achieving that goal.

